    {
      "@id": "https://windswept.energy/ontology/mppt_law_principle",
      "class": "DesignPrinciple",
      "canonical_name": "",
      "definition": "The design rule that the controller tracks the operating point that maximises power. The kite-turbine form: the controller sets the generator torque demand so the rotor settles where the power coefficient against tip speed ratio peaks, without demanding more than the wind can deliver.",
      "symbol": "",
      "dimension": "",
      "unit_si": "",
      "unit_paper": "",
      "strict_synonyms": [
        "power-maximizing control"
      ],
      "related_terms": [],
      "sources": [
        "AWEC2026_Power_Efficiency_Analysis_of_a_Rotary_Kite_Airborne_Wind_Energy"
      ],
      "constraints": [],
      "status": "ruled",
      "source": "standard-physics"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://windswept.energy/ontology/n_lines_tradeoff",
      "class": "DesignPrinciple",
      "canonical_name": "",
      "definition": "The design rule that the number of blade lines or polygon sides carries a trade: more lines smooth the torque ripple and share the load but add drag, mass and tether cost; fewer cost less but load each line harder and roughen the torque. The minimum of three reads a geometric reason: two lines cannot close a ring, and three is the first stable polygon that holds a ring shape under load.",
      "symbol": "",
      "dimension": "",
      "unit_si": "",
      "unit_paper": "",
      "strict_synonyms": [
        "n_lines_tradeoff"
      ],
      "related_terms": [
        "Number of blades tradeoff: power vs complexity",
        "recommendations for future model selection"
      ],
      "sources": [
        "AWEC2019_Comparison_of_Engineering_Induction_Models_in",
        "AWEC2019_Practical_Experimentation_on_Rotary_Network_AWES",
        "BUILD AND TEST A 3 KILOWATT PROTOTYPE OF A COAXIAL, MULTI-ROTOR WIND TUR",
        "Dynamic analysis of a rotorcraft TFM_Javier_Carceller_Final",
        "Tulloch, PhD Thesis Final Submission"
      ],
      "constraints": [],
      "status": "ruled",
      "source": "standard-physics"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://windswept.energy/ontology/radius_scaling_principle",
      "class": "DesignPrinciple",
      "canonical_name": "",
      "definition": "The design rule that machine performance and mass scale with rotor radius as power laws, and that the exponent is a claim to be stated and sourced, not a universal constant. The recognised texts read the common usage: power scaling follows the square of radius through the swept area, mass scaling follows the cube of radius through geometric similarity.",
      "symbol": "",
      "dimension": "",
      "unit_si": "",
      "unit_paper": "",
      "strict_synonyms": [
